          "title": "ASME Battlebots",
          "subtitle": "Oct 2009 - Jun 2013",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Associated with UCLA"
                },
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Through being a member of Battlebots at UCLA for 4 years, I had the chance to dream up, design, and build combat robots to fight in international competition. After the initial brainstorming phase for each robot, I used SolidWorks to model up components and build assemblies, perform stress analysis to analyze the designs, and generate engineering drawings. These drawings were then brought down to the student machine shop where I manually machined components (using mill, lathe, etc.). After final assembly, we competed against hobbyists and professionals alike in the battle arena, where our robots ultimately came out either scratched or as scrap, ready for the next design iteration.\n\nAs president of the organization in my senior year I also managed daily ASME activities, including meetings, events, finances, and projects, including the expansion of the Battlebots team scope from solely 60lb combat robots to 1lb, 3lb, and 15lb weight classes."
                }
